{
  "name": "happy",
  "integration": "audit-events-to-webhook",
  "script": "audit-events-to-webhook/audit-events-to-webhook.star",
  "description": "The full outbound path, asserted on the RECEIVER: this integration emits no assets, so the only evidence it ran is the request that arrived at the webhook. Events come from /api/v1.0/account/events.json with the fixed `created:<1h` search and an account-token bearer; they are then POSTed to dst_url. Rows carry the real models.Event shape -- created_at/processed_at are unix ints not RFC3339 strings, id/client_id/organization_id/site_id/source_id/target_id are always present as UUID strings (the zero UUID for an account-scoped event, not null or omitted), success is a bool, and details is a free-form object whose web-request enricher fills ip/ua/path/method/user_id/user_email. Three assertions carry the weight. First, dst_url must be POSTed to VERBATIM: unlike src_url it is not a console base, and a webhook is almost always path-plus-token, so reading it with get_url_base() would drop the path and deliver every event to the wrong place -- requests_absent on POST / catches that. Second, the body is NEWLINE-DELIMITED JSON, one event object per line, which is what the script actually builds; the `}\\n{` assertion pins the separator so a change to a JSON array cannot pass silently as a receiver-breaking change. Third, and most important, the runZero ACCOUNT TOKEN must never reach the third-party webhook: the script builds a fresh header map for the destination leg, and requests_absent on a POST carrying that token is the regression test for anyone who later reuses the source headers to save a line.",

